{
  "$schema": "https://opencode.ai/config.json",
  "command": {
    "translate": {
      "description": "Translate fcitx5-online vue-i18n localization files",
      "template": "Load the translate skill first. The argument is: $ARGUMENTS\n\nIf no argument was provided, print ONLY this usage text and stop:\n\nUsage: /translate <mode>\n  sync     Scan src for new t() strings, add missing keys to src/locales/zh-CN.json, translate them to Simplified Chinese, then propagate the new keys to every other locale file\n  all      Translate all other languages using the English keys + Simplified Chinese as context\n  <lang>   Add a new language or translate an existing one (e.g. ru)\n\nThe canonical English strings are the KEYS of each JSON locale file (src/locales/*.json). The English locale itself is auto-generated in src/i18n.ts and has no file. Never change keys when translating — only values.\n\nIf the argument is \"sync\", first collect the current set of message keys from the t() function in the sources:\nnode .opencode/skills/translate/keys.mjs\nNote that keys used with the t() imported from fcitx5-config-vue (in StatusArea.vue) belong to that package's own locales and must NOT be added here. Compare the collected keys against the keys of src/locales/zh-CN.json. Add any new key to src/locales/zh-CN.json as \"English sentence\": \"<Chinese translation>\" (translate it). Then, in every other src/locales/<lang>.json file, add the same new keys with their translated values. Report a summary.\n\nIf the argument is \"all\", do NOT run sync. Read src/locales/zh-CN.json (Simplified Chinese) and use the keys as English context, then translate every other locale registered in the messages object of src/i18n.ts except 'en' (currently just ru). Only translate values; never change the keys.\n\nIf the argument is a language code like ru, ja, ko, or zh-CN, check whether it is registered in the messages object of src/i18n.ts independently of whether src/locales/<lang>.json exists. If src/locales/<lang>.json exists, do an incremental translation: compare its keys to src/locales/zh-CN.json, translate any missing keys, and fix untranslated values (values still equal to their key). If that existing file is not yet registered in src/i18n.ts (no messages entry for the locale code), add \"import <alias> from './locales/<lang>.json'\" where <alias> is an identifier-safe binding of the locale code (e.g. zh_CN for zh-CN) and point the messages key \"<lang>\" at <alias>, keeping the original locale code as the key. If the file does not exist, create it by copying src/locales/zh-CN.json, register it in src/i18n.ts with the same import/entry pattern, then translate the whole file.\n\nWhenever you add or register a locale in the messages object, you MUST also map that locale in the getNaiveLocale() function of src/i18n.ts so the strings inside naive-ui components are localized too. Import the matching NLocale from 'naive-ui' and add \"<lang>\": <NLocale> to the mapping object (e.g. ru -> ruRU, zh-CN -> zhCN, ja -> jaJP, vi -> viVN, zh-TW -> zhTW). If naive-ui has no locale for that language (e.g. he, ka, ca), map it to enUS as a fallback.\n\nAfter translating, validate EVERY locale registered in the messages object of src/i18n.ts except 'en':\nnode .opencode/skills/translate/validate.mjs src/locales/zh-CN.json src/locales/<lang>.json\nDerive the list of locales to validate from the keys of the messages object in src/i18n.ts, excluding only 'en' and the source catalog 'zh-CN' (fall back to the src/locales/*.json files if messages cannot be parsed). Run it for each remaining registered locale, including any newly added ones. Any MISSING/EXTRA key or UNTRANSLATED value must be fixed before finishing."
    }
  }
}
